From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shupeng Xue <dspxue@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 11:29:02 +1100
Subject: [PATCH wireless-next 15/19] wifi: b43: deliver RX and TX status
 through NAPI for BCM4331 HT

b43 passes every received frame to mac80211 from its threaded IRQ
handler with ieee80211_rx_ni(), outside of NAPI, so received TCP
segments never go through GRO. On a BCM4331 (Mac mini, 5 GHz HT40,
MCS 15, -21 dBm, short range) TCP receive stays at 199-206 Mbit/s,
while the proprietary wl driver reaches 212 Mbit/s on the same link.

Add an opt-in NAPI path, enabled with the new htphy_napi module
parameter (default off). It is only set up for the configuration it was
tested in: a BCM4331 on BCMA over PCIe with the HT-PHY, htphy_11n >= 2,
qos4331=0 (so all traffic uses the shared BE FIFO) and DMA. Other
devices, PIO and separate per-AC FIFOs keep the existing direct
delivery.

The IRQ thread still does all hardware access under wl->mutex. Instead
of calling mac80211 directly, it queues received frames and finished TX
statuses, in completion order, on one FIFO, and schedules a NAPI
instance on a dummy netdev. The poll function hands them to
ieee80211_rx_napi() and ieee80211_tx_status_skb() without taking
wl->mutex. skb->pkt_type tags the queued skbs, as mac80211 does for its
own frame queue.

The FIFO needs bounds:
- Received frames are dropped once 4096 skbs are queued.
- TX statuses are never dropped, since mac80211 needs them for rate
  control, status reporting and freeing the frames. Instead, TX
  submission is paused once 2048 statuses are queued: mac80211's queues
  are stopped, and b43_dma_tx() and b43_dma_tx_ampdu() return -EBUSY,
  on which b43_tx_work() requeues the frames. While paused, only frames
  already on the BE and multicast rings, at most 256, can add statuses.
  The poll function schedules tx_work when the count drops to 512,
  which lifts the pause under wl->mutex. Existing ring wakes and the
  core init/start wakes leave a pause in place.
- NAPI is enabled after core init has chosen DMA or PIO and before the
  IRQ is requested. It is disabled after the IRQ is freed, and the
  queue is purged then.

NAPI alone does not help. The IRQ thread typically queues one or a few
frames per poll, and when the poll completes, napi_complete_done()
flushes GRO. A trace showed that nearly every napi_gro_receive() call
returned GRO_HELD and was flushed at the end of the same poll, without
merging. Throughput stayed at 199 Mbit/s. With a GRO flush timeout,
held packets wait for the following polls and most of them merge. The
timeout is 125 us. The dummy netdev is never registered and has no
sysfs knob, so set its gro_flush_timeout before netif_napi_add(), which
copies it into the NAPI instance.

Results on that link (TCP, one client and AP, short range), measured
with a functionally equivalent version of this series on Linux 6.18.54;
this exact version was only build-tested:
- 2 min receive A/B/A: 237.12 Mbit/s with NAPI and the timeout, 199.11
  without the timeout, 235.74 with it again.
- 30 min receive: 234.72 Mbit/s, no TX FIFO underflows. SSH 30/30,
  ping loss 0.17%.
- 30 min transmit: 168.59 Mbit/s, unchanged.
- Reconnecting 3 times, a controller restart, s2idle and S3 resume:
  233-236 Mbit/s afterwards.
- QoS smoke test with BE, BK, VI and VO marked traffic plus SSH: pass.
- With the TX thresholds temporarily lowered to 8 and 2 to force
  backpressure: 20719 pauses and no stall.

Limitations: tested on one device and near range only. The PIO path
and the other eligibility exclusions are not exercised; they keep the
old delivery. The 4096-skb RX limit and the 125 us timeout are not
tuned beyond this setup. This does not address A-MPDU session drain
or epochs; statuses of a stopped BA session can still be processed
after a new session on the same TID starts.

